A||||8||wilburt||Tail wiggle (long)||||5||
Z||000000||wilburt||05-06-2000||06:24 PM||||Just completed a forum search, and can't find anything close to the symptoms I've got on an '86 2M4 auto.  It feels like an out-of round tire, except it's not up and down 'bouncy', it's more of a back and forth shake, wobble. It only happens at 32 to 40 MPH,(most noticeable under power) then again at 68-74 MPH. I believe it's coming from the drivers rear, 'cause it's more pronounced turning right, and I can't feel anything in the steering wheel. It's not clicking, or clunking, but I believe its a halfshaft problem. I've replaced: control arm bushings, ball joints, tie rod ends, wheels, tires, struts and shocks, Grand Am brakes. So is it the halfshaft, or the rear wheel bearing hubs?  Thank-you for your time! ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||05-06-2000||08:08 PM||||I still think it's a bad tire. The belts can shift sideways. Will make the whole back of the car shake from side to side. Someone following you should be able to see it. Try jacking up each rear corner of the car. Turn the wheels by hand and watch for any left-to right movement of the tread. Anything other than completely straight is a problem. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||wilburt||05-06-2000||09:13 PM||||Thanks Raydar, while I did put other tires and wheels on, they were used, but in excellent condition. I'll rotate and balance them tomorrow, inspect them as you suggested, and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-07-2000||03:09 PM||||does the tranny have a huge bracket with one mount or 2 mounts, one front & one rear?<P>Has the dog bone been replaced? what kind of dog bone was installed?<P>GM made a bracket change to a single mount for 86.5. This setup has a few problems. One of them is that when an OE torque reaction strut is worn the engine/tranny set can oscilate at certain speeds and engine loads. (In this setup, the OE dogbone can allow as much as 2(Two) inches of engine motion when even a little worn.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||wilburt||05-07-2000||09:08 PM||||Thanks Ogre. Less than 2k ago I replaced the two engine mounts, and the dog bone with stock pieces. That is about the time I noticed the very slight wiggle. It's worse now, but it feels kinda like the engine/tranny ARE wobbling around together, causing the shimmy.  I had the tires balanced and rotated today, and I also checked the run-out on the wheels like Raydar suggested and everything is good there. I even tried to duplicate the problem by running it up to speed on jack stands, but  to no avail.  Perhaps I should take my deck lid off and have a passenger see if there is engine shake at that speed? Anyway, I will pry on the mounts tomorrow, and hope one is broke. Thanks again! ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-07-2000||11:04 PM||||Here are some other areas to check, Cradle Bushings and bolts, Strut tower bolts, A-arm bushings, Wheel Alignment with the corrected specs. Some shops use older equipment and don't have the updated specs from the TSB's.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||05-07-2000||11:26 PM||||Here is my $.02<P>While you are checking the other items, try checking the rear wheel hubs, one or both may be worn.  If the wheel hub is worn, then you will have a wiggle in the bearings.<P>You will most notice wheel hub wear when making turns while driving. Your description of being "more pronounced turning right" leads me to believe it is a hub bearing problem.<P>The best way to check for wear is to raise the fiero, grab the wheel assembly with both hands, (one hand at the 12, and one hand at the 6 o'clock position).<P>Try to rock the wheel assembly if there is pronounced movement the wheel hub needs to be replaced.<P>The wheel hub is a sealed bearing assembly.  They are fairly easy to replace being bolted on with 3 bolts and the hub nut.<P>NOTE<BR>The hub nut is 30mm or there abouts.<P>After removing the hub nut(s), make sure you purchase a new hub nut(s) - DO NOT reuse the old one(s).<P>If you replace the wheel hub(s), you will have to have your alignment checked.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 05-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dan86gt3.8||05-08-2000||10:32 AM||||i HAD THE SAME DAMN PROBLEM after I busted my passenger side axle.  Part of mine was the steering rack was bad, my dad replaced it while I was at school and the took most mof mine out, but I still got a little wobble going on at like 60-65+||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-08-2000||10:59 AM||||I take it that 2 mounts means there was only one under the tranny......<P>The car feels like it's wobbling sideways or like someone is dancing on the brake?<P>If it feels like brake tapping, it's likely engine oscilation. The low frequency of it may make it hard to observe even with the lid off. (I think the frequency is 10-20 cycles a sec.)<P>I doubt you'll find a broken mount. Likely the OE dog bone is already getting crappy, especially if it's the type 2 one. (Type 2 have round bushings that fill the loops.)<P>If you pull out the side of the bushing on the type 2 so you can see under it, you'll likely find it's already getting chewed up. (The bushing is shaped like 2 cones facing each other. It's not tube shaped.) Neither type of strut can take the load the single tranny mount gives them, but the newer type are worse than the old style. (I tore one of the new ones up in less than 15,000miles.)<P>I fixed mine by installing a type 1 bone stuffed with polly bushings. (Type 1 bones have a ridge around the out side of the loops, and the bushings don't fill the loops completely.) That's cheaper and looks pretty stock with black polly. You can also get the steel one Fiero store has if you don't have an old type 1 bone and can't get one.<P>((Note: this particular problem only happens with 86.5 & up 4 cylinders with automatic. The 2 engine/tranny mounts are under the center line of the engine tranny set. They hold the set up. They don't do any torque damping at all. This is unlike the 3-4 mount setup used on other motors and trannys. In those setups you get significant tourqe damping in the motor/tranny mounts because of mount placement. I'll be posting a thing about this in the new cave updates.))<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
